Most modern application can be divided into three layers.  
1>Data/Adapters Layer - The layer that is responsible for data storage or interaction with other systems. This layer mainly consists of repositories that can each contain one or more data sources. In this layer, our coroutines interact with other libraries, including some that might not support them.
2>Domain Layer - The layer where our application business logic is implemented. It includes classes that encapsulate specific operations, like use cases, services, facades, etc. In this layer, coroutines are used to optimize our core processes.
3>Presentation/API/UI layer - The layer that starts our application’s processes. It might be considered an entry to our application. In this layer, we start coroutines and handle their execution results.
Each of those layers has different coroutine use cases. 
Data/Adapters Layer is where we implement repositories, providers, adapters, data sources, etc. This layer is relatively easy nowadays because many popular JVM libraries support Kotlin Coroutines either out of the box or with some additional dependencies. 
When a callback is called, the coroutine should be resumed using the resume method on Continuation. If this callback function is cancellable, it should be cancelled inside the invokeOnCancellation lambda expression. Callback functions that allow us to set separate functions for success and failure can be implemented in a few different ways. We could wrap over callback functions and return Result, and either resume our coroutine with Result.success or with Result.failure.Another option is to return a nullable value and either resume our coroutine with the result data or with the null value. The last popular option is to resume with a result in the case of callback function success or resume with an exception in case of failure. In the latter case, the exception is thrown from the suspension point.
In Kotlin Coroutines, we use threads with great precision, and blocking them is a big problem. If we block the thread from Dispatchers.Main on Android, our whole application freezes. If we block the thread from Dispatchers.Default, we can forget about efficient processor use. This is why we should never make a blocking call without first specifying the dispatcher. When we need to make a blocking call, we should specify the dispatcher using withContext. Inmost cases,whenwe implement repositories in applications, it is enough to use Dispatchers.IO. But  Dispatchers.IO is limited to 64 threads which may not be enough for the backend. So we will be usinng limitedParellism on the Dispatcher.IO to make another threasd whose thread is mmore than 64. When we suspect a function is being called by many Coroutine that they may use a lot of thread, we have to use Dispatcher independent of Dispatcher.IO. We should also remember to use Dispatcher.Default for CPU Intensive opperation and Dispatchher.Main.immediate for anythhing that change the main view.
Suspending functions are perfect for representing the process of producing/fetching a single value. However, when we expect more than one value, we should use Flow instead. When we fetch a single value from an API, it is best to use a suspend function; however, when we set up a WebSocket and listen for messages, we should use Flow instead. To create such a flow, we should use callbackFlow orchannelFlow. End builder with awaitClose.Flow can also be used for other callback functions, and it should be used when these callbacks might produce multiple values.If you need to use a specific dispatcher in a flow builder, use flowOn on the produced flow.
Domain Layer is the layer we should avoid operating on coroutine scope objects and exposing suspending functions. The layer below this (the presentation layer) is responsible for starting coroutines on scope objects. In the domain layer, we should use coroutine scope functions to start coroutines.
When we want two processes to happen in parallel, we should wrap our function body with coroutineScope and use async builder inside it to start each process that should run asynchronously. modification should only make these two processes run in parallel. When we want to start two asynchronous processes and then await their completion, we can do that by creating a new coroutine for each of them using the async function; however, the same result can also be achieved if we start only one process using async and run the second one on the same coroutine. The following implementation of produceCurrentUserPar will have practically the same behavior as the previous one.We can use async together with collection processing functions to start an asynchronous process for each list element.  to limit the number of concurrent calls, we can use a rate limiter. We transform our list into Flow and then use flatMapMerge with the concurrency parameter, which specifies how many concurrent calls will bbe sent. Any Exception in Children will break the parents if we use CoroutineScope. 
The last layer is the Presentation layer, which is where coroutines are typically launched. In some types of applications, this layer is easiest because frameworks like Spring Boot or Ktor do the entire job for us.  We can use the CoroutineWorker class and implement its doWork method to specify what should be done by a task. This method is a suspend function, so it will be started in a coroutine by the library, therefore we don’t need to do this ourselves.However, in some other situations we need to start coroutines ourselves. For this we typically use launch on a scope object. 
When you do not have a library or class that can start a coroutine or create a scope, you might need to make a custom scope and use it to launch a coroutine. We define a custom coroutine scope using the CoroutineScope function. Inside it, it is practically standard practice to use SupervisorJob. 
Instead of starting coroutines on a scope object, we can also use the runBlocking function, which starts a coroutine and blocks the current thread until this coroutine is finished. Therefore, runBlocking should only be used when we want to block a thread. The two most common reasons why it is used are:
1. To wrap the main function. This is a correct use of runBlocking, because we need to block the thread until the coroutine started by runBlocking is finished.
2. To wrap test functions. In this case, we also need to block the test thread, so this test doesn’t finish execution until thecoroutine completes.